WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:09.676
Your fleet makes a billion traces a day, but you can only store one percent. How do you keep the ones with errors? Tail sampling.

00:00:10.876 --> 00:00:11.805
Pause and think.

00:00:12.205 --> 00:00:14.817
Why can't head sampling keep all error traces?

00:00:15.217 --> 00:00:22.532
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:28.913
This video explains how large fleets keep the important traces without storing everything.

00:00:30.113 --> 00:00:32.029
Think of a hospital triage desk.

00:00:32.429 --> 00:00:36.585
Head sampling decides at the door, before anyone knows what is wrong.

00:00:36.985 --> 00:00:40.677
Tail sampling waits until the whole visit is over, then decides.

00:00:41.077 --> 00:00:44.966
Serious cases, errors and slow requests, are always kept.

00:00:45.366 --> 00:00:50.126
A fixed share of healthy visits is kept too, so you know what normal looks like.

00:00:51.326 --> 00:00:52.522
Here's the pipeline.

00:00:52.922 --> 00:00:56.626
Applications emit spans through the OpenTelemetry SDK.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:09.308
New word: S D K. Software Development Kit: a bundle of libraries, tools and docs for building on a platform. Picture it like this: A toolbox that comes with a new piece of furniture.

00:01:09.708 --> 00:01:18.950
New word: OpenTelemetry. The open standard and tools for traces, metrics and logs. Picture it like this: A common language for monitoring.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:25.887
A load balancer sends every span of one trace to the same collector, by hashing the trace ID.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:33.256
New word: I D. Identifier: a unique name or number for a thing. Picture it like this: A name tag.

00:01:34.856 --> 00:01:39.175
The gateway buffers spans until the root span ends or a timeout passes.

00:01:39.575 --> 00:01:49.106
Then it decides. Errors are kept. Slow traces are kept. Otherwise a hash of the trace ID picks a fixed percentage, so every collector makes the same choice.

00:01:49.506 --> 00:01:51.213
Kept traces go to storage.

00:01:52.413 --> 00:01:52.936
Recap.

00:01:53.336 --> 00:01:57.120
Tail sampling needs every span of a trace on one collector.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.121
Always keep errors and slow traces.

00:02:00.521 --> 00:02:03.888
Hash-based sampling gives the same decision everywhere.

00:02:04.288 --> 00:02:08.491
Cap label combinations per metric so one team can't explode your bill.

00:02:08.891 --> 00:02:12.919
Practice in lesson thirty nine point three and design sd twenty seven.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.387
Back to the question.

00:02:15.787 --> 00:02:18.399
Why can't head sampling keep all error traces?

00:02:18.799 --> 00:02:22.770
It decides at the start, before anyone knows the request will fail.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:24.840
Quick recap.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.098
Head sampling decides early.

00:02:27.498 --> 00:02:30.308
Tail sampling decides after the trace ends.

00:02:30.708 --> 00:02:32.739
Keep errors and slow traces.
